WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.759 --> 00:00:10.779
The five assignments build one map across the term. These pages explain the arc, the rooms, the dates, and the rules, so the blank page never wins.

2
00:00:10.779 --> 00:00:21.781
Start with the overview. Five assignments, each worth 20 percent: notice, interpret, investigate, repair, integrate. Together they build your Personal Cartography.

3
00:00:21.781 --> 00:00:32.784
Note the two deadlines. First half due October 25; second half due December 11. Everything is due by 11:59 p.m. Eastern Time, and nothing is due in the final week.

4
00:00:32.784 --> 00:00:43.786
Open one assignment room at a time. Each room shows the purpose, what to submit, the marking criteria, and your program connection. Separate pages cover how stronger work grows, release dates, AI use, and the FAQ.

5
00:00:43.786 --> 00:00:54.565
Set your program lens for examples. The lens tailors the rooms and starter questions to your field. It never changes the prompt, the grading, or the due dates.

6
00:00:54.565 --> 00:01:05.570
Open the Assignment Start Lab when you want a working session. Fixed course rules, no AI, build a private start plan you can print or save. For Judgment Calls, the lab gives readiness steps only; that assessment is a closed, timed Blackboard sitting.

7
00:01:05.571 --> 00:01:16.574
Build a first-draft plan, then submit on Blackboard, always. The starter page turns your answers into a Word planning document. The official assignment, rubric, and submission all live on Blackboard.

8
00:01:16.574 --> 00:01:22.394
Nothing here is submitted or graded. Blackboard is the only submission channel.

9
00:01:22.394 --> 00:01:27.263
Where next: Block one hour, open the lab, and leave with a plan.
